(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-2-vascular-lymphatics-quiz"]={"title":"Arteries, Veins, and Lymphatic Systems — Anatomy Exam 2","category":"Anatomy","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"What is the fundamental directional difference between arteries and veins?","choices":["Neither vessel type has a consistent direction of flow","Arteries bring blood away from the heart; veins bring blood toward the heart","Both vessel types carry blood in the same direction","Arteries bring blood toward the heart; veins bring blood away from the heart"],"answer":1,"exp":"Arteries bring blood away from the heart to other parts of the body (systemic circulation), while veins bring blood toward the heart. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"What are the smallest arteries called, and what are the smallest veins called?","choices":["Neither vessel type has a specific name for its smallest branches","Both are called capillaries","Venules (smallest arteries); arterioles (smallest veins)","Arterioles (smallest arteries); venules (smallest veins)"],"answer":3,"exp":"The smallest arteries are called arterioles, and the smallest veins are called venules. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Do arteries have pulses? Do veins?","choices":["Arteries have pulses; veins do not","Both have identical, palpable pulses","Neither vessel type has a palpable pulse","Veins have pulses; arteries do not"],"answer":0,"exp":"Arteries have pulses, while veins do not have pulses. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"How do the vessel walls of arteries compare to veins in terms of thickness and smooth muscle?","choices":["Veins have thicker walls with more smooth muscle than arteries","Neither vessel type contains smooth muscle","Both vessel types have identical wall thickness and smooth muscle content","Arteries have thicker walls with more smooth muscle than veins"],"answer":3,"exp":"Arteries have thicker walls than veins, and arteries have more smooth muscle than veins. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"When compressed (e.g., with ultrasound), how do arteries behave compared to veins, and why is this clinically important?","choices":["Arteries maintain their shape when compressed; veins do not — important for procedures like central line placement to differentiate vein from artery","Veins maintain their shape when compressed; arteries collapse instead — irrelevant to central line placement technique","Both vessel types behave identically when compressed, each one fully maintaining its round shape under any amount of applied pressure","Compression has no clinical relevance whatsoever for vessel identification during central line placement"],"answer":0,"exp":"When compressed, arteries maintain their shape while veins do not; this is clinically important, for example when placing a central line in the internal jugular vein (near the carotid artery), to ensure the correct vessel is accessed and the patient does not bleed out. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What are the three layers of vessel walls (except capillaries), from innermost to outermost?","choices":["Intima, media, adventitia","Adventitia, media, intima","Endothelium, media, and pericardium","Only two layers: media and adventitia"],"answer":0,"exp":"The three layers of vessel walls (except capillaries), from innermost to outermost, are the intima (tunica intima), media (tunica media), and adventitia (tunica adventitia). — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What is the vasa vasorum?","choices":["A type of heart valve located within the aortic root itself, regulating one-way blood flow","Blood vessels within the walls of the thickest vessels, supplying the vessel wall itself","The innermost layer of a blood vessel, composed entirely of endothelial cells","A type of lymphatic vessel found only within the walls of the thoracic duct"],"answer":1,"exp":"The vasa vasorum are blood vessels contained within the walls of the thickest vessels, supplying the vessel wall itself. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What composes the intima (tunica intima), and what is a key function of the media (tunica media)?","choices":["Both intima and media are composed of identical connective tissue involved in nutrient exchange processes","Intima consists of smooth muscle; media is composed of the vessel's endothelium instead","Neither layer has any described composition or function within the vessel wall itself","Intima is composed of the endothelium; media consists of smooth muscle involved in vasoconstriction and vasodilation"],"answer":3,"exp":"The intima (innermost layer) is composed of the endothelium of the vessel, while the media (middle layer) consists of smooth muscle, involved in vasoconstriction and vasodilation. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What does the adventitia (tunica adventitia) do?","choices":["Consists of smooth muscle controlling vasoconstriction","Fibers of the adventitia anchor the blood vessels","Produces blood cells","Composed of the endothelium, lining the vessel lumen"],"answer":1,"exp":"The adventitia (outermost layer) has fibers that anchor the blood vessels. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What is a key difference in the endothelial lining and elastic membranes of arteries versus veins?","choices":["Neither vessel type has any elastic membrane or pleated endothelial folds present in their walls at all","Arteries have pleated endothelial folds and elastic membranes (internal/external) in their intima/media; veins lack both features","Veins have pleated endothelial folds and elastic membranes within their intima/media; arteries lack both of these features","Both vessel types have identical endothelial linings and elastic membranes throughout their intima and media"],"answer":1,"exp":"Arteries have pleated folds in their endothelial lining and thin elastic membranes in their media (external elastic membrane) and intima (internal elastic membrane), while veins lack both of these features. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Which vessel type has one-way valves, and why is this clinically relevant to varicose veins?","choices":["Valves are found equally in both the arteries and the veins, and are entirely unrelated to varicose vein development","Both vessel types have identical valve structures, present equally throughout their entire vessel length","Arteries have one-way valves; veins do not, which is why varicose veins never actually develop in veins","Many veins have one-way valves (arteries do not); varicose veins can occur due to weakened walls, damaged valves, or increased blood pressure"],"answer":3,"exp":"Many veins have one-way valves to prevent backflow of blood (arteries do not); varicose veins can occur due to weakened walls, damaged valves, increased blood pressure, and other factors. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"What are elastic arteries, and what are examples given?","choices":["Vessels lacking any media layer entirely, found only within the pulmonary circulation as small, thin-walled capillary beds near the alveoli","The smallest arteries, about 30 microns in diameter, controlling blood flow directly into individual capillary beds throughout the tissues","Large vessels up to 2.5 cm in diameter with elastic membranes, tolerating pressure changes; examples include the aorta, brachiocephalic trunk, and pulmonary trunk","Medium-sized vessels up to 0.4 cm in diameter, lacking elastic membranes entirely; examples include the radial and ulnar arteries"],"answer":2,"exp":"Elastic arteries are large vessels up to 2.5 cm in diameter, containing elastic membranes in the media and intima, very resilient and tolerant of pressure changes during the cardiac cycle (recoil during diastole helps propel blood forward); examples include the aorta, brachiocephalic trunk, and pulmonary trunk. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What are muscular arteries, and what controls their diameter?","choices":["The smallest arteries, about 30 microns in diameter, controlling blood flow directly into the capillaries","Large vessels up to 2.5 cm in diameter with a thin media; the diameter is fixed and entirely uncontrolled by any nerves","Vessels found only within the heart itself, supplying the myocardium exclusively through small branches","Medium-sized arteries up to 0.4 cm diameter with thicker media than elastic arteries; diameter is under control of the autonomic nervous system"],"answer":3,"exp":"Muscular arteries are medium-sized arteries up to 0.4 cm in diameter, with thicker media than elastic arteries; their diameter is under control of the autonomic nervous system. Examples include the radial, ulnar, external carotid, brachial, femoral, and mesenteric arteries. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What are arterioles, and what is their function?","choices":["Smallest arteries (~30 microns diameter) with very thin adventitia, controlling blood flow between arteries and capillaries","Vessels found only within the venous system, controlling the blood flow between venules and the larger veins","Vessels with no smooth muscle at all in their media, measuring up to 2.5 cm in diameter","The largest arteries, up to 2.5 cm in diameter, possessing a thick, well-developed adventitia layer"],"answer":0,"exp":"Arterioles are the smallest arteries, around 30 microns in diameter, with very thin adventitia and media that may have incomplete smooth muscle layers; they control blood flow between arteries and capillaries. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What is distinctive about capillary structure, and what are the three types described?","choices":["Smallest of all vessels (~8 microns diameter), lacking adventitia and media; three types are continuous, fenestrated, and sinusoids","Only one type of capillary exists: continuous, each possessing a thick adventitia and media layer","Largest of all vessels, with a thick adventitia and media, measuring about 2.5 cm in diameter","Capillaries are identical in structure to the arterioles, each retaining a complete media and adventitia layer"],"answer":0,"exp":"Capillaries are the smallest of all vessels, about 8 microns in diameter (about the diameter of a red blood cell), and lack adventitia and media; they are the only vessels where nutrient/waste exchange occurs between blood and tissues, and there are three types: continuous (complete endothelial lining, most common), fenestrated (incomplete lining with pores), and sinusoids (discontinuous, with pores and large gaps). — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"What characterizes venules, and what is their function?","choices":["The largest veins, such as the superior and inferior vena cava, possessing a thick tunica media layer","Veins with the thickest tunica media of any vessel type, collecting blood from larger veins","Veins found only within the upper extremities, possessing a thick, well-developed tunica media","Smallest of the veins, collecting blood from the capillaries, lacking or having only thin tunica media"],"answer":3,"exp":"Venules are the smallest of the veins, collecting blood from the capillaries; they lack or have only a thin tunica media. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What characterizes medium-sized veins, and what are examples given?","choices":["Media is the largest layer, contains no elastic fibers, and lacks valves entirely; examples include venules only","No distinguishable layers are present at all, and no examples of this vein type are ever given in this course material","Adventitia (tunica externa) is the largest layer, contains elastic fibers, has one-way valves; examples include the radial, tibial, and popliteal veins","Adventitia is by far the thinnest layer, with no valves present anywhere at all; examples include the superior and inferior vena cava"],"answer":2,"exp":"Medium-sized veins have an adventitia (tunica externa) that is the largest of the layers, containing elastic fibers, with one-way valves present; they are found alongside muscular arteries, with examples including the radial vein, tibial vein, and popliteal vein. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Why do many medium veins have venous valves, and what is their function?","choices":["To produce hormones that regulate blood pressure throughout the entire systemic venous circulation continuously","To increase the blood flow away from the heart toward the capillary beds of the lower extremities, directly and continuously","Valves have no functional purpose in the venous system at all, existing only as vestigial evolutionary structures","To help blood in the lower extremities return to the heart against gravity; valves compartmentalize blood, acting as one-way valves to prevent backflow"],"answer":3,"exp":"Venous valves help blood returning to the heart from the lower extremities go against gravity; these valves compartmentalize the blood in the veins, acting as one-way valves that prevent backflow of blood. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"What characterizes large veins, and what are examples given?","choices":["Media is by far the thickest layer with abundant smooth muscle; intima is thin; valves are present; examples include the radial and tibial veins","Large veins are identical in structure to arterioles, with a thin adventitia and a thick, muscular smooth muscle media layer throughout","Intima is by far the thickest layer, with abundant valves present; examples include the radial and popliteal veins","Adventitia is the thickest layer (with some smooth muscle); intima and media are very thin; no valves present; examples include the superior and inferior vena cava"],"answer":3,"exp":"Large veins have the thickest adventitia (containing some smooth muscle), with very thin intima and media, and no valves; examples include the superior vena cava and inferior vena cava. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What regions does the superior vena cava receive blood from, and what is the one exception to veins draining into the venae cavae?","choices":["Receives from the abdomen only; hepatic veins are the sole exception, draining via the portal vein instead","Receives from the head, neck, chest, shoulders, and upper limbs; cardiac veins are the exception (they drain via the coronary sinus instead)","Receives from the lower extremities only; the renal veins are the sole exception, draining via the aorta instead","There are no exceptions whatsoever; all veins, including cardiac veins, drain directly into the venae cavae"],"answer":1,"exp":"The superior vena cava receives blood from the head, neck, chest, shoulders, and upper limbs; all veins drain into the superior or inferior vena cava except cardiac veins, which drain via the coronary sinus into the right atrium directly. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"What is one described sequence for venous return from the hand to the heart via the cephalic vein?","choices":["Cephalic vein → digital veins → subclavian vein → superior vena cava directly, skipping the palmar arches","Digital veins → basilic vein → axillary vein → subclavian vein → the inferior vena cava directly, bypassing the heart's right side","Palmar venous arches → cephalic vein → the aorta → right atrium, entirely bypassing the venae cavae and subclavian vein","Digital veins → superficial palmar veins → palmar venous arches → cephalic vein → subclavian vein → brachiocephalic vein → superior vena cava"],"answer":3,"exp":"One venous return sequence is: digital veins, superficial palmar veins, palmar venous arches, cephalic vein, subclavian vein, brachiocephalic vein, and superior vena cava. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"What connects the cephalic vein to the basilic vein at the elbow, in the alternate venous return pathway?","choices":["The axillary vein directly","The brachial artery","The median cubital vein","The median antebrachial vein"],"answer":2,"exp":"The median cubital vein connects the cephalic vein to the basilic vein, in the pathway where superficial palmar veins drain into the cephalic vein, then to the basilic vein, axillary vein, subclavian vein, brachiocephalic vein, and superior vena cava. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"What does the azygos vein drain, and what does it drain into?","choices":["Drains into the inferior vena cava exclusively, collecting only from the left kidney","Drains the esophageal and right intercostal veins, draining into the superior vena cava","Drains only the left intercostal veins, emptying instead into the hemi-azygos vein","Drains the entire lower extremity venous system, emptying into the inferior vena cava"],"answer":1,"exp":"The azygos vein drains the esophageal and right intercostal veins, and along with the brachiocephalic veins, drains into the superior vena cava. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"What do the hemi-azygos and accessory hemi-azygos veins drain?","choices":["The entire upper extremity venous system","Right intercostal veins","Left superior intercostal veins","The kidneys directly"],"answer":2,"exp":"The hemi-azygos and accessory hemi-azygos veins drain the left superior intercostal veins. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"What veins drain into the brachiocephalic veins?","choices":["Only the hepatic veins drain directly into this one vessel","Subclavian veins, azygos, hemi-azygos, and internal thoracic veins","Only the femoral vein drains directly into this vessel","Only the renal veins drain directly into this vessel"],"answer":1,"exp":"Subclavian veins, the azygos vein, hemi-azygos vein, and internal thoracic veins all drain into the brachiocephalic veins. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"What does the inferior vena cava collect, and what is unique about the left versus right gonadal vein drainage?","choices":["Collects most venous blood from organs inferior to the diaphragm; the right gonadal vein drains directly into the IVC, while the left gonadal vein drains into the left renal vein first","Collects blood only from the head and neck region; both gonadal veins drain directly into the internal jugular vein on each respective side","Both gonadal veins drain identically and directly into the IVC, without ever passing through either renal vein or the liver first","The inferior vena cava drains into the left atrium, collecting blood only from those upper body organs lying above the diaphragm and from the heart itself"],"answer":0,"exp":"The inferior vena cava collects most of the venous blood from organs inferior to the diaphragm (via common iliac veins, lumbar veins, gonadal veins, hepatic veins, and other abdominal veins); the right gonadal vein drains directly into the inferior vena cava, while the left gonadal vein drains into the left renal vein, which then drains into the inferior vena cava. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"What is the venous return pathway from the foot's plantar veins to the inferior vena cava?","choices":["Plantar veins → femoral vein → the inferior vena cava directly, skipping the popliteal and iliac veins entirely","Popliteal vein → plantar veins → femoral vein → external iliac vein → common iliac vein → the aorta directly, bypassing the vena cava","Plantar veins → anterior/posterior tibial and fibular veins → popliteal vein → femoral vein → external iliac vein → common iliac vein → inferior vena cava","Femoral vein → plantar veins → popliteal vein → common iliac vein → the right atrium directly, entirely bypassing the IVC"],"answer":2,"exp":"Blood from the sole and digits drains to plantar veins and the ventral plantar arches, then into the anterior tibial, posterior tibial, and fibular veins, then the popliteal vein, femoral vein, external iliac vein, common iliac vein, and finally the inferior vena cava. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"What are the great and small saphenous veins, and where do they ultimately drain?","choices":["Veins that drain directly into the inferior vena cava, entirely bypassing both the femoral vein and popliteal system altogether","Superficial veins draining the dorsal venous arch of the foot, ultimately reaching the femoral vein (great saphenous) and popliteal region (small saphenous) before the deeper system","Deep veins found only within the thigh region, running alongside the femoral artery and draining directly and exclusively into the popliteal vein","Arteries supplying the foot with oxygenated blood via branches of the femoral and the popliteal arteries directly, entirely unrelated to any venous drainage"],"answer":1,"exp":"The great saphenous vein and small saphenous vein are superficial veins draining the dorsal venous arch of the foot; the great saphenous vein reaches the femoral vein (which also receives the deep femoral vein), continuing on to the external iliac vein, common iliac vein, inferior vena cava, and right atrium. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"Which veins drain into the internal iliac vein, and where does the median sacral vein drain instead?","choices":["Gluteal and obturator veins both drain into the external iliac vein instead; the median sacral vein drains into the right common iliac vein","All pelvic veins drain directly into the aorta itself, including the median sacral vein, with no exceptions ever occurring","Gluteal, internal pudendal, obturator, and lateral sacral veins drain into the internal iliac vein; the median sacral vein drains into the left common iliac vein","The median sacral vein drains into the internal iliac vein, exactly the same as the gluteal and obturator veins"],"answer":2,"exp":"Gluteal veins, internal pudendal veins, obturator veins, and lateral sacral veins drain into the internal iliac vein (then the common iliac vein, then the inferior vena cava); the median sacral vein is an exception, draining into the left common iliac vein instead. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"What does the anterior tibial artery form in the foot, and what does the posterior tibial artery form?","choices":["Anterior tibial artery forms the plantar arteries; posterior tibial artery instead forms the dorsalis pedis artery","Neither artery contributes to foot circulation at all, which is instead supplied entirely by the femoral artery","Both arteries together form the identical dorsalis pedis artery, with no plantar artery contribution at all","Anterior tibial artery forms the dorsalis pedis artery; posterior tibial artery forms the medial and lateral plantar arteries"],"answer":3,"exp":"The anterior tibial artery forms the dorsalis pedis artery (a common site for taking pulses), while the posterior tibial artery forms the medial and lateral plantar arteries. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 47"}]};
